<p>
  You can delete any of these at any time by clearing site data in your browser settings. Browser storage is
  per-site, so the first two are cleared under <code>tzortzoglou.eu</code> and the third under
  <code>app.tzortzoglou.eu</code>. Clearing the theme key simply resets that site to its default appearance;
  clearing the Firebase entry logs you out.
</p>

<h2 id="choice">3. Your analytics choice</h2>

<p id="consent-state" class="font-semibold"></p>
<p>
  <button type="button" id="consent-withdraw" class="btn-secondary px-5 py-2 text-sm">Turn analytics on</button>
</p>
<p id="consent-note" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="text-sm"></p>
<noscript>
  <p><strong>JavaScript is disabled, so no analytics runs at all.</strong> Nothing is being counted.</p>
</noscript>

<p>
  You are asked once, on your first visit. <strong>Analytics stays off unless you actively accept</strong> — declining
  is one click, exactly as easy as accepting, and nothing on the site changes either way. Your answer is remembered in
  your browser under <code>analytics-consent</code> and never leaves your device.
</p>

<h3>What is counted if you accept</h3>
<p>
  <strong>This changed on 18 September 2026.</strong> A counted page view used to carry no identifier at all. It now
  carries a visitor number: a 64-bit hash of a secret my server holds, your IP address, your browser's
  User-Agent string and which of my two sites you were on. A new secret replaces the old one every 24 hours and the
  old one is destroyed, so two page views from your browser on the same day share a number, and tomorrow's number is
  a different one with nothing left that could join it to today's.
</p>
<p>
  The counter is mine. Accepting sends one short message to <strong>api.tzortzoglou.eu</strong>, the same server that
  receives the contact form; no analytics company is involved and no third-party script is loaded.
</p>
<p><strong>What a counted page view stores:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>the number described above</li>
  <li>the <strong>path</strong> of the page, without its query string or fragment</li>
  <li>which of my two sites it was</li>
  <li>the <strong>host</strong> of the page that linked you here (<code>www.linkedin.com</code>, never the full
    address)</li>
  <li>the <strong>time</strong></li>
  <li>a coarse browser name, a coarse operating system name, whether you are on a phone, a tablet or a desktop, and
    which of a handful of width brackets your window falls into</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What reaches my server and is not stored:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>your <strong>IP address</strong>, an input to the hash and a rate-limiter key, held in memory both times</li>
  <li>your full <strong>User-Agent string</strong>, the other input taken from your browser</li>
  <li>the full referring URL, which my server reads a host off and then drops, because a full referring address can
    carry someone's search terms in its query string</li>
  <li>your exact window width, which my server rounds into one of the brackets above and then drops</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Nothing in the second list reaches the stored row. There is still no cookie and no fingerprint read off your
  hardware, fonts or canvas, and a request whose User-Agent identifies it as a bot is not counted at all. The visitor
  number is erased from the row 30 days after the page view, which leaves the counts and removes the one field that
  ties one view to another. Until September 2026 this counting was done by GoatCounter instead; nothing goes there
  now, and what it gathered is frozen. The <a href="/privacy/#analytics">Privacy Policy</a> sets out the legal basis
  and what the number does and does not make possible.
</p>
<p>
  This choice covers this site. The signed-in application at <code>app.tzortzoglou.eu</code> counts its own page loads
  for everyone, without asking, because that is a service you deliberately logged in to, not an audience being
  measured. It stores the same fields, the number included, and still not which account was signed in.
  <a href="/privacy/#app-analytics">Section 3.2 of the Privacy Policy</a> explains why it is treated differently, and
  what the number means inside an audience of a handful of approved accounts.
</p>

<h3>Why the banner only asks about this one thing</h3>
<p>
  Under Article 5(3) of the ePrivacy Directive (in Sweden, the Electronic Communications Act,
  <span lang="sv">lag (2022:482) om elektronisk kommunikation</span>), consent is required before storing or reading
  information on your device — <em>except</em> where it is strictly necessary for a service you explicitly asked for.
</p>
<p>
  The theme preference and the login token both fall inside that exemption, so asking permission for them would be
  asking a question with only one honest answer. The page counter is different: it is not necessary for anything, so it
  is the one thing genuinely worth your consent, and the one thing the banner controls. A banner whose buttons changed
  nothing would be theatre, and regulators across the EU have repeatedly acted against exactly that pattern.
</p>
<p>
  Because analytics now runs on consent, not on legitimate interest, you can also withdraw it at any time using
  the button above — as easily as you gave it (Art. 7(3) GDPR).
</p>
<p>
  If this site ever adds anything else that requires consent — advertising, cross-site analytics, an embedded YouTube or
  Maps frame, a marketing pixel — it will be added to this same mechanism <strong>before</strong> it goes live.
</p>

<h2 id="third-parties">4. Third parties your browser contacts</h2>
<p>
  Some assets are loaded from other providers instead of this domain. Your browser connects to them directly, so they
  receive your IP address, user-agent, and the page you were viewing. None of them set cookies here, but the connection
  itself is a transfer of data, so it is listed in full. The page-view counter is no longer among them: it is a request
  to my own server, and <strong>only if you accepted it</strong>. A country code for counted page views is planned and
  is not in place yet; when it is, the lookup will read a MaxMind database file sitting on that same server, so your
  browser will still not be contacting anyone new.
</p>

